[38;2;124;124;124mAnd $R$ is unitary on $L^2(A, h)$ (using traciality of $h$ and the fact that $R$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpreserves $h$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\|S\| = 1$, and the answer is no.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, but let me reconsider once more. Is it really true that $R$ preserves $h$? [0m[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this from the definition.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe unitary antipode $R$ is a *-anti-automorphism. For compact quantum groups, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mit's known that $h \circ R = h$. This follows from the uniqueness of the Haar [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstate and the fact that $h \circ R$ is also a left and right invariant state [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(since $R$ intertwines the coproduct in the appropriate way).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me verify: if $\Delta(a) = \sum a_{(1)} \otimes a_{(2)}$, then $(R[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\otimes R) \circ \Delta \circ R = \Delta^{op}$, i.e., $R$ reverses the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoproduct. More precisely, $\Delta(R(a)) = (R \otimes R)(\Delta^{op}(a))$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLeft invariance of $h$: $(\text{id} \otimes h)\Delta(a) = h(a) 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf we define $h'(a) = h(R(a))$, then:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$(\text{id} \otimes h')\Delta(a) = (\text{id} \otimes h)(\text{id} \otimes [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mR)\Delta(a)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $(\text{id} \otimes R)\Delta(a) = \sum a_{(1)} \otimes R(a_{(2)})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, this doesn't directly simplify. Let me use a different approach.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the standard result is that $h \circ R = h$. This is proven in e.g. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mWoronowicz's original paper or in Timmermann's book. The key insight is that $R$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmaps left-invariant functionals to right-invariant ones and vice versa, but [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msince $h$ is both left and right invariant, $h \circ R = h$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo yes, $h \circ R = h$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, my proof is correct: for Kac-type compact quantum groups, $\|S\| = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1$, and $\|S\| = 2$ is impossible.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, one more thing to verify: I claimed that in the Kac case, the scaling [0m[38;2;124;124;124mgroup is trivial. This is indeed a defining property. A compact quantum group is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof Kac type if and only if:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. The Haar state is tracial, or equivalently[0m
